          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            We are interested in the local structure of zeros of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+function"} analytic function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
            as well as in analogues, e.g. in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+analytic+geometry"} rigid analytic geometry
          %p
            In one variable, a holomorphic function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 0
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(z)=(z-z_0)^n u(z)
            where
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u(z_0)\neq 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            is holomorphic and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} nonnegative integer
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+varieties"} algebraic varieties
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+space"} projective space
            are algebraic.
          %p
            The Weierstrass preparation theorem and related facts (Weierstrass division theorem and Weierstrass formula) provide the most basic relations between
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial"} polynomial
          %p
            Let
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\geq 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            complex coordinates
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = (z_1,\ldots,z_{n-1})
            and the
            = succeed "-th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = f(z_1,\ldots, z_{n-1},w)
            vanishing at origin
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(0,\ldots, 0)=0
            = succeed "-axis." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
          %h2#weierstrass_polynomial Weierstrass polynomial
          %p
            The
            %strong Weierstrass polynomial
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            is a polynomial of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi w
                    %mi d
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mi w
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mi …
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  w^d + a_1(z) w^{d-1}+\ldots+a_d(z),\,\,\,\,\,a_i(0)=0.
          %p
            The integer
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em the degree of the Weierstrass polynomial
          %h2#weierstrass_preparation_theorem_in_
            Weierstrass preparation theorem in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            be a function which is holomorphic in some neighborhood of origin
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\in\mathbb{C}^n
            and not identically equal to zero on the
            = succeed "-axis." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is
            %em uniquely
            representable in the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mi P
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f = P\cdot h
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a Weierstrass polynomial of degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(0) \neq 0
          %h2#weierstrass_division_theorem Weierstrass division theorem
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{n,a}
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+ring"} local ring
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/germs"} germs
            of holomorphic functions at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in\mathbb{C}^n
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi n
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_n:=\mathcal{O}_{n,0}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g=g(z,w)\in\mathcal{O}_{n-1}[w]
            be a Weierstrass polynomial of degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\in\mathcal{O}_n
            can be represented as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi h
                  %mo +
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f = g\cdot h+r
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo =
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r = r(z,w)
            is a polynomial of degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt k
          %p
            As a corollary, if another function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            vanishes on the zero set of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            divides
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_n
          %h2#ingredients_of_proofs Ingredients of proofs
          %p
            Weierstrass’s original result considered the ring of holomorphic functions, and therefore used analytic methods such as the
            %span.newWikiWord
              residue theorem
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/residue+theorem"} ?
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+integral+formula"} Cauchy integral formula
            are used. Analogous results are now known in the ring of formal power series, as well as for power series over non-Archimedean fields; in these settings, the proof can be made fully algebraic.
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Named after
